    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-43079",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Suppose $C$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $det(C)=-126.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}:=\\frac{8}{21}{R}_{5}.$ What is the value of $det(D)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The row operation applied to $C$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{8}{21}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\frac{8}{21}det(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{21}\\cdot (-126) \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
